{"id":"cryptography-basics","title":"Cryptography basics","subject":"Cybersecurity","label":"Cybersecurity","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":173,"duration":173.14,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":460,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"The puzzle","start":0,"end":15.545},{"id":"explain","title":"Encryption","start":15.545,"end":55.185},{"id":"explain2","title":"Hashing and HTTPS","start":55.185,"end":91.925},{"id":"example","title":"Caesar example","start":91.925,"end":107.425},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":107.425,"end":114.555},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":114.555,"end":120.385},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":120.385,"end":150.445},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":150.445,"end":173.14}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":6.47,"text":"You type your password into a website, and it travels across the world. Why doesn't someone just read it?","w":[0.52,0.7,0.94,1.1,1.6,1.84,1.92,2.84,2.98,3.12,3.58,3.98,4.1,4.96,5.12,5.42,5.76,6.02,6.26]},{"s":0,"a":6.92,"b":15.12,"text":"I'm Kora, and in three minutes I'll show you the three ideas that keep that journey safe: encryption, hashing, and HTTPS.","w":[7,7.2,8.04,8.16,8.3,8.56,8.94,9.16,9.36,9.52,9.64,9.92,10.32,10.48,10.7,10.88,11.24,12.16,13.08,13.7,14.02]},{"s":1,"a":15.97,"b":23.64,"text":"Start with encryption. It scrambles a readable message, called plaintext, into unreadable ciphertext, using a key.","w":[16.08,16.42,16.6,17.72,17.86,18.38,18.48,18.88,19.64,19.94,20.94,21.3,21.84,22.92,23.2,23.28]},{"s":1,"a":23.94,"b":27.28,"text":"Only someone with the right key can turn it back into plaintext.","w":[24.06,24.38,24.76,24.94,25.1,25.38,25.64,25.82,26.04,26.16,26.4,26.62]},{"s":1,"a":27.58,"b":33.58,"text":"The Caesar cipher shifts every letter by a fixed number. It is easy to break, but it shows the idea.","w":[27.6,27.78,28.24,28.7,29.22,29.5,29.8,29.98,30.06,30.36,31.06,31.18,31.4,31.64,31.76,32.36,32.52,32.66,32.98,33.16]},{"s":1,"a":33.88,"b":37.73,"text":"Symmetric encryption uses one secret key to lock and unlock.","w":[34.02,34.54,35.16,35.6,35.82,36.22,36.48,36.66,37.04,37.22]},{"s":1,"a":38.03,"b":42.87,"text":"It is fast, but both sides must share that key safely, and that is the hard part.","w":[38.06,38.18,38.34,39.14,39.34,39.64,40.04,40.24,40.5,40.7,40.98,41.8,41.92,42.16,42.26,42.38,42.64]},{"s":1,"a":43.17,"b":47.75,"text":"Asymmetric encryption, also called public key encryption, uses a pair of keys.","w":[43.24,43.8,44.48,44.8,45.12,45.5,45.7,46.64,46.9,46.96,47.2,47.3]},{"s":1,"a":48.05,"b":54.76,"text":"Anyone can lock a message with your public key, but only your private key unlocks it. That solves the sharing problem.","w":[48.14,48.54,48.74,49.02,49.1,49.46,49.62,49.78,50.12,50.8,51.06,51.3,51.5,51.9,52.18,52.64,53.28,53.58,53.92,54.04,54.38]},{"s":2,"a":55.61,"b":57.71,"text":"Hashing is different. It is one way.","w":[55.64,56.06,56.18,56.92,57.04,57.26,57.42]},{"s":2,"a":58.01,"b":60.58,"text":"It turns any data into a fixed length fingerprint.","w":[58.06,58.14,58.5,58.7,59.1,59.36,59.46,59.78,60.1]},{"s":2,"a":60.88,"b":65.08,"text":"The same input always gives the same hash, and you cannot work backwards from it.","w":[60.9,61.06,61.38,61.78,62.08,62.32,62.48,62.74,63.48,63.6,63.74,64.12,64.38,64.76,64.98]},{"s":2,"a":65.38,"b":69.01,"text":"Websites should store password hashes, with a salt, not passwords.","w":[65.44,65.94,66.16,66.46,66.88,67.26,67.44,67.52,68.2,68.4]},{"s":2,"a":69.31,"b":74.78,"text":"A salt is extra random data mixed in, so two people with the same password get different hashes.","w":[69.38,69.54,69.94,70.18,70.5,70.86,71.14,71.42,72.02,72.22,72.42,72.74,72.88,72.98,73.22,73.7,73.92,74.24]},{"s":2,"a":75.08,"b":77.83,"text":"Hashes also check that a file was not changed.","w":[75.14,75.7,76.02,76.32,76.48,76.62,76.96,77.12,77.34]},{"s":2,"a":78.13,"b":81.57,"text":"And HTTPS? It uses both kinds of encryption.","w":[78.18,78.52,79.94,80.1,80.38,80.68,80.96,81.08]},{"s":2,"a":81.87,"b":87.31,"text":"Public key cryptography and a certificate prove the site's identity and agree a shared secret key.","w":[81.88,82.26,82.48,83.32,83.46,83.52,84.14,84.44,84.56,84.86,85.7,85.84,86.28,86.36,86.74,87.08]},{"s":2,"a":87.61,"b":91.5,"text":"Then fast symmetric encryption protects everything you send.","w":[87.7,88.08,88.48,88.96,89.48,90.02,90.4,90.58]},{"s":3,"a":92.35,"b":95.23,"text":"Let's try the Caesar cipher. Take the word CAT.","w":[92.4,92.64,92.88,93.04,93.44,94.32,94.52,94.64,94.96]},{"s":3,"a":95.53,"b":101.45,"text":"Shift each letter forward by one. C becomes D, A becomes B, T becomes U.","w":[95.62,95.96,96.2,96.48,96.84,97.12,97.76,98.02,98.42,99.3,99.46,99.84,100.56,100.8,101.26]},{"s":3,"a":101.75,"b":103.95,"text":"You get DBU, and the key is one.","w":[101.76,101.9,102.06,103.02,103.12,103.26,103.52,103.74]},{"s":3,"a":104.25,"b":107,"text":"To reverse it, shift each letter back by one.","w":[104.26,104.42,104.86,105.26,105.62,105.84,106.14,106.46,106.78]},{"s":4,"a":107.85,"b":110.37,"text":"Quick check. Is hashing the same as encryption?","w":[107.9,108.16,108.66,108.82,109.22,109.38,109.7,109.82]},{"s":4,"a":110.82,"b":111.75,"text":"Think for a moment.","w":[110.88,111.12,111.28,111.36]},{"s":4,"a":112.05,"b":114.13,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[112.08,112.4,112.66,112.78,113.1,113.56,113.74]},{"s":5,"a":114.98,"b":119.96,"text":"No. Encryption can be reversed with the right key, but hashing cannot be reversed at all.","w":[115.08,115.8,116.38,116.52,116.64,117.04,117.18,117.3,117.5,118.2,118.36,118.82,119.1,119.24,119.66,119.78]},{"s":6,"a":120.81,"b":125.3,"text":"Three common mistakes. First, thinking encoding or hashing is encryption.","w":[120.84,121.18,121.54,122.72,123.22,123.56,124.1,124.22,124.64,124.78]},{"s":6,"a":125.6,"b":129.15,"text":"Encoding is just a format change, and hashing is one way.","w":[125.66,126.22,126.34,126.6,126.7,127.12,127.82,127.98,128.46,128.7,128.86]},{"s":6,"a":129.45,"b":135.49,"text":"Second, thinking the padlock means a site is trustworthy. It only means the connection is encrypted.","w":[129.54,130.44,130.8,130.94,131.64,131.9,132.04,132.38,132.5,133.54,133.72,133.98,134.26,134.36,134.88,135]},{"s":6,"a":135.79,"b":141.89,"text":"Anyone can buy a certificate. If a site asks for a password over plain HTTP, walk away.","w":[135.88,136.26,136.44,136.64,136.72,137.78,137.9,138.02,138.44,138.74,138.9,139,139.58,139.8,140.38,141.36,141.62]},{"s":6,"a":142.19,"b":146.49,"text":"Third, inventing your own cipher. Use proven, published methods.","w":[142.22,143,143.48,143.7,143.94,144.78,145,145.5,145.96]},{"s":6,"a":146.79,"b":150.02,"text":"And never touch a system without permission. That is illegal.","w":[146.86,147.06,147.34,147.58,147.7,148.04,148.32,149.26,149.46,149.58]},{"s":7,"a":150.87,"b":155.07,"text":"Three things to remember. Encryption scrambles, and a key reverses it.","w":[150.9,151.14,151.4,151.54,152.38,152.98,153.9,154.04,154.16,154.48,154.96]},{"s":7,"a":155.37,"b":158.3,"text":"Hashing is a one way fingerprint that cannot be reversed.","w":[155.44,155.88,156,156.16,156.32,156.56,157.02,157.18,157.56,157.74]},{"s":7,"a":158.6,"b":163.64,"text":"HTTPS combines public key and symmetric encryption to keep your data private.","w":[158.76,159.52,160.04,160.38,160.98,161.12,161.6,162.34,162.48,162.68,162.84,163.14]},{"s":7,"a":163.94,"b":168.58,"text":"Top tip: check for the padlock, but never trust a site just because it has one.","w":[163.96,164.24,164.9,165.14,165.28,165.38,166.16,166.38,166.68,167,167.1,167.5,167.74,168.02,168.12,168.44]},{"s":7,"a":168.88,"b":172.54,"text":"Thanks for learning with me here at Knowzone. See you in the next lesson.","w":[168.88,169.22,169.4,169.7,169.88,170.06,170.38,170.5,171.38,171.58,171.74,171.82,171.94,172.2]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"The puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Password crosses the world — why unread?"},{"at":6.82,"kind":"text","text":"3 ideas: encryption, hashing, HTTPS"}]},{"at":15.72,"heading":"Encryption basics","items":[{"at":15.87,"kind":"term","text":"Encryption","detail":"Scrambles plaintext into ciphertext using a key"},{"at":23.84,"kind":"text","text":"Only the right key reverses it"},{"at":27.48,"kind":"text","text":"Caesar cipher: fixed shift; easy to break"}]},{"at":33.63,"heading":"Symmetric vs asymmetric","items":[{"at":33.78,"kind":"term","text":"Symmetric encryption","detail":"One secret key locks and unlocks"},{"at":37.93,"kind":"text","text":"Fast; both sides must share the key safely"},{"at":43.07,"kind":"term","text":"Asymmetric encryption","detail":"Public key encryption uses a pair of keys"},{"at":47.95,"kind":"text","text":"Public key locks; private key unlocks"}]},{"at":55.36,"heading":"Hashing","items":[{"at":55.51,"kind":"term","text":"Hashing","detail":"One-way: cannot be reversed"},{"at":57.91,"kind":"text","text":"Any data → fixed-length fingerprint"},{"at":60.78,"kind":"text","text":"Same input → same hash; cannot reverse"},{"at":65.28,"kind":"text","text":"Store password hashes with a salt, not passwords"},{"at":69.21,"kind":"term","text":"Salt","detail":"Extra random data mixed in before hashing"}]},{"at":77.88,"heading":"HTTPS","items":[{"at":78.03,"kind":"text","text":"HTTPS uses both kinds of encryption"},{"at":81.77,"kind":"text","text":"Certificate + public key prove identity; agree shared key"},{"at":87.51,"kind":"text","text":"Symmetric encryption protects your data"}]},{"at":92.1,"heading":"Caesar example","items":[{"at":92.25,"kind":"text","text":"Word: CAT"},{"at":95.43,"kind":"step","text":"C→D, A→B, T→U"},{"at":101.65,"kind":"formula","text":"CAT +1 → DBU"},{"at":101.65,"kind":"formula","text":"key = 1"},{"at":104.15,"kind":"formula","text":"DBU −1 → CAT"}]},{"at":107.42,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":107.42,"kind":"question","text":"Is hashing the same as encryption?"}]},{"at":114.73,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":114.88,"kind":"text","text":"No."},{"at":114.88,"kind":"text","text":"Encryption reverses with key; hashing cannot."}]},{"at":120.56,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":120.71,"kind":"wrong","text":"Encoding or hashing is encryption"},{"at":129.35,"kind":"wrong","text":"Padlock means a site is trustworthy"},{"at":135.69,"kind":"text","text":"Anyone can buy a certificate; plain HTTP = walk away"}]},{"at":141.94,"heading":"Mistakes 3 & law","items":[{"at":142.09,"kind":"wrong","text":"Invent your own cipher"},{"at":146.69,"kind":"text","text":"Never touch a system without permission — illegal"}]},{"at":150.62,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":150.77,"kind":"text","text":"Encryption scrambles; a key reverses it"},{"at":155.27,"kind":"text","text":"Hashing is a one-way fingerprint; cannot reverse"},{"at":158.5,"kind":"text","text":"HTTPS: public key + symmetric encryption"},{"at":163.84,"kind":"tip","text":"Check for the padlock, but never trust a site just because it has one"}]}],"check":{"at":114.38,"seconds":10,"question":"Is hashing the same as encryption?","answer":"No. Encryption can be reversed with the right key, but hashing cannot be reversed at all."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}